WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:02.795
COLT MCANLIS: In this episode
of Cloud Performance Atlas,

00:00:02.795 --> 00:00:05.880
I help some VR expert with
their disk performance.

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:08.792
Does this VR headset make
my performance look awesome?

00:00:08.792 --> 00:00:09.750
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:15.060
VR Ignition is a
company specializing

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:19.050
in the processing of content
for VR and AR applications.

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:22.050
Their product can scale to
handle massive point clouds

00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:25.479
across thousands of CPUs using
the Google Cloud Platform

00:00:25.479 --> 00:00:27.270
and, due to the nature
of their algorithms,

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:30.390
had a large need for
local SSDs to handle

00:00:30.390 --> 00:00:32.259
their out-of-core algorithms.

00:00:32.259 --> 00:00:34.167
However, they were
confused, because they

00:00:34.167 --> 00:00:36.000
weren't getting the
type of read performance

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:38.820
they were expecting from
these high performance disks.

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:41.230
I'll spare you some of
the debugging details,

00:00:41.230 --> 00:00:42.690
but it turned out
that the problem

00:00:42.690 --> 00:00:44.460
was with their disk interface.

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:47.910
See, for local SSDs, there's
two primary interfaces--

00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:50.160
SCSI and NVMe.

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:52.770
SCSI, of course, we've
all seen for years now

00:00:52.770 --> 00:00:57.150
and is the default for most disk
interfaces in our data centers.

00:00:57.150 --> 00:01:00.300
NVMe, however, is the new
kid on the block designed

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:03.570
with modern technologies in mind
to provide higher performance.

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:07.200
For example, whereas SCSI
has one queue for commands,

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:10.440
NVMe is designed to have
up to 64,000 queues.

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:14.520
Moreover, each of those queues
can in turn have up to 64,000

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:17.350
commands simultaneously.

00:01:17.350 --> 00:01:19.950
Now, you can switch what
interface your machine uses

00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:22.110
at creation time
by passing along

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:25.015
the interface tag to
the local SSD param,

00:01:25.015 --> 00:01:26.640
and to show the
performance difference,

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:29.280
we can see the two head-to-head.

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:32.160
We clearly see the NVMe has
an advantage with respect

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:34.170
to read performance
and a slight advantage

00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:36.990
over write performance,
but here's the catch.

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:40.650
In order to use NVMe, you
have to have a disk image that

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:42.180
supports it, and
this was the thing

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:44.250
that was hurting VR Ignition.

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:46.320
Firstly, they weren't
setting the flag.

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:49.830
And secondly, they weren't using
a custom image for their builds

00:01:49.830 --> 00:01:52.650
that didn't have NVMe support.

00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:55.376
Thankfully, after a decent
amount of technical discussion,

00:01:55.376 --> 00:01:57.750
they were able to move to a
public image that was already

00:01:57.750 --> 00:01:59.970
optimized for NVMe
which let them get

00:01:59.970 --> 00:02:01.777
the big boost in performance.

00:02:01.777 --> 00:02:03.610
If you'd like to know
more about this topic,

00:02:03.610 --> 00:02:05.430
check out the article
linked in the description

00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:07.530
below, and don't forget to
check out the rest of the Cloud

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:08.729
Performance Atlas content.

00:02:08.729 --> 00:02:10.330
Because when it
comes to performance,

00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:11.579
every millisecond counts.

00:02:11.579 --> 00:02:14.929
